-- allium: 3
--
-- Human solving — a fallible, bounded attempt and its assessment
--
-- Scope
--   The fourth of four models of a player, and the one in which a limit
--   changes what is noticed, kept in mind and got wrong, by chance.
--   reach.allium, effort.allium and lapse.allium model the same player
--   without chance and none of the four imports another.
--   Simulate a person solving fresh classic Sudoku from its givens, and
--   assess difficulty relative to a declared profile and environment over
--   repeated seeded attempts. An attempt includes looking without finding,
--   remembering, forgetting, writing notes, faulty reasoning and recovery.
--   Research motivates the mechanisms, not the numerical defaults. The
--   evidence and calibration limits are in explanation/human-solving.md.
--
-- Includes
--   - Independent cognitive ability, learned expertise, biases and coping.
--   - Separate physical sheet, bounded mental state, and observer judgement.
--   - Explicit microsteps, effort, provenance, seeds and finite budgets.
--   - Manual/automatic notes, highlighting, feedback and uniqueness premise.
--   - Named pattern recognition, bounded elementary reasoning, and guesses.
--   - Within-attempt fatigue, frustration and rest, with fixed learned skills.
--   - Four provisional experience presets on one standard ability.
--   - The projection from this module's description of a player to
--     technique.allium's Profile, so that one player can be put through
--     reach, effort and lapse as well.
--
-- Excludes
--   - Runtime/UI implementation, persistence, puzzle generation and hints.
--   - Importing in-progress games, Sudoku variants, or learning across games.
--   - Diagnoses, intelligence scores, predictions in minutes, calibrated
--     population probabilities, and a single universal difficulty number.
--   - An exhaustive expert repertoire or an unbounded solution search.
--   - Changing reach, effort or lapse into this model, or this one into
--     them. Each answers differently what a limit does, several of their
--     open questions name the answer given here, and they stay without
--     chance.
--   - The random generator. Draws come from this game's randomness port;
--     what this module states is how a draw becomes a decision.
--
-- Dependencies
--   sudoku.allium owns the rules; technique.allium owns logical techniques
--   and the Profile this module projects onto. It keeps no technique/Grid:
--   a Grid has one set of marks and licenses whatever they support, where a
--   person has a sheet and a mind that may disagree and finds only what
--   they look for.
--   An exact evaluator may establish well-posedness and judge an attempt.
--   Its solution and deductions are never player inputs. An explicitly
--   enabled feedback aid may reveal only the queried entry's error signal.
--
-- Vocabulary
--   technique.allium's words stand: technique, deduction, placement, strike,
--   candidate, mark, load, proof, witness. Attempt is this model's run, one
--   seeded solve from the givens to a stopping reason, and microstep its
--   step; they have names of their own because a microstep is far smaller
--   than a deduction and most of them deduce nothing. Sheet, what is
--   physically written, against mind, what is believed; neither is
--   technique.allium's grid. Entry, a digit standing on the sheet, a given or
--   a placement the player wrote. Note, one cell's written marks together
--   with a claim of coverage; a mark is still one written candidate. Fact, a
--   proposition the player has acquired, which may be false; chunk, facts
--   held as one. A coverage fact is the player's running possibility set for
--   one cell or one digit in one unit, held as one item and grown by folding
--   other facts into it. Observer, the judge that sees the solution and
--   never steers.
--   Capacity here counts retained chunks, fatigue is a state between zero and
--   one, and patience counts fruitless episodes; technique.allium's Profile
--   uses the same three words for coarser figures, and the projection below
--   says how they meet.

use "./sudoku.allium" as sudoku
use "./technique.allium" as technique

enum ActionKind {
    attend | inspect | recall | rehearse | write_note | erase_note
    | recognize | infer | verify | enter | erase_entry | guess
    | backtrack | rest | claim_finished | give_up
}
enum FactKind {
    entry | empty | possible | excluded | coverage | unique_promise
}
enum SourceKind { board | manual_note | automatic_note | highlight | feedback | reasoning }
enum Highlighting { off | peers | same_digit | peers_and_digit }
enum Feedback { none | conflicts | solution_check }
enum StopReason { claimed | abandoned | no_action | effort_limit | microstep_limit }
enum ObjectiveResult { solved | incomplete | erroneous }
enum ReasoningMode { familiar_pattern | elementary | speculative }
enum Obstacle {
    unnoticed | forgotten | incomplete_information | capacity_limit
    | unfamiliar_pattern | reasoning_limit | fixation | incorrect_belief
    | branch_limit | patience_limit | budget_limit | catalogue_limit
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Inputs and tunable parameters
------------------------------------------------------------

-- All ranges below are inclusive. Probabilities are dimensionless in [0,1];
-- zero disables that source of chance. Positive integer bounds are finite.
value CognitiveAbility {
    capacity: Integer             -- 1..12 simultaneous chunks
    retention: Integer            -- 1..1000 microsteps without refresh
    interference: Decimal         -- forgetting probability per focus change
    attention_cells: Integer      -- 1..9 positions in the current focus
    switch_effort: Integer        -- 0..20 effort added to a focus change
    reasoning_depth: Integer      -- 0..24 implications per reasoning episode
    notice: Decimal               -- probability of noticing an inspected fact
    perception_error: Decimal     -- misreading a noticed fact
    inference_error: Decimal      -- faulty attempted logical step
    entry_error: Decimal          -- wrong entry/erasure/note operation
}

value Skill {
    method: technique/Technique
    fluency: Decimal              -- recognition probability when premises are ready
    chunk_facts: Integer          -- 1..12 atomic facts per learned pattern chunk
    habit: Decimal                -- 0.1..10 relative search weight
}

value Experience {
    familiar: Set<technique/Technique>
    fluency: Decimal
    chunk_facts: Integer
    habit: Decimal
    overrides: Set<Skill>
    -- One override per method, only for familiar methods. Effective skill is
    -- the override, else the common figures above for a familiar method.
    -- An unfamiliar method has no recognition shortcut and chunk_facts=1.
}

value Biases {
    area_fixation: Decimal        -- 0..10 extra weight for current focus
    digit_fixation: Decimal       -- 0..10 extra weight for current digit
    familiar_preference: Decimal  -- 0..10 extra weight times skill fluency
    confirmation: Decimal        -- chance of skipping disconfirming evidence
    overconfidence: Decimal      -- confidence increment, reduces verification
}

value Coping {
    note_use: Decimal             -- probability of choosing to externalize a fact
    verification: Decimal        -- probability of checking a proposed entry
    confidence_threshold: Decimal -- perceived confidence required to commit
    guess_when_stuck: Decimal     -- chance of opening a branch at patience limit
    guess_depth: Integer          -- 0..9 standing branches; 0 disables guesses
    guess_limit: Integer          -- 0..100 guesses in the whole attempt
    branch_recall: Decimal        -- probability of correctly recalling an unwritten change
    patience: Integer             -- 1..1000 consecutive fruitless search episodes
    rest_limit: Integer           -- 0..100 rests
    fatigue_gain: Decimal         -- 0..1 fatigue per unit of non-rest effort
    frustration_gain: Decimal     -- 0..1 per fruitless episode/detected error
    progress_relief: Decimal      -- 0..1 frustration removed on perceived progress
    rest_relief: Decimal          -- 0..1 removed from fatigue and frustration per rest
}

value PlayerProfile {
    name: String
    ability: CognitiveAbility
    experience: Experience
    biases: Biases
    coping: Coping
}

value Environment {
    manual_notes: Boolean
    automatic_candidates: Boolean
    highlighting: Highlighting
    feedback: Feedback
    uniqueness_disclosed: Boolean
    branch_notes: Boolean
}

value EffortCosts {
    read: Integer                -- 1..100, inspect/recall/rehearse/attend
    think: Integer               -- 1..100, recognize/infer/verify
    write: Integer               -- 1..100, one entry or one note edit
    rest: Integer                -- 1..100
    declare: Integer             -- 1..100, claim_finished/give_up
}

value Limits {
    effort: Integer              -- 1..1000000
    microsteps: Integer           -- 1..1000000
}

value AttemptRequest {
    givens: Set<sudoku/Given>
    profile: PlayerProfile
    environment: Environment
    costs: EffortCosts
    limits: Limits
    seed: Integer                -- 0..2147483647
    model_version: String
    catalogue_version: String
    random_version: String
}

-- The acting policy receives no givens or evaluator state. Geometry is
-- public; puzzle contents enter only through a requested observation.
value PolicyInput {
    profile: PlayerProfile
    environment: Environment
    costs: EffortCosts
    limits: Limits
    seed: Integer
    model_version: String
    catalogue_version: String
    random_version: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Physical record, mental record and observation
------------------------------------------------------------

value Note {
    position: sudoku/Position
    possible: Set<Integer>
    excluded: Set<Integer>
    complete: Boolean
    -- complete is the player's assertion that all nine alternatives were
    -- checked. It may be wrong or stale; the observer does not repair it.
}

value BranchMark {
    branch: Integer
    position: sudoku/Position
    previous_digit: Integer?
    previous_note: Note?
    -- A deliberately written checkpoint fragment, not a hidden snapshot.
}

value Sheet {
    givens: Set<sudoku/Given>
    entries: Set<technique/Placement>
    notes: Set<Note>
    branch_marks: Set<BranchMark>
}

value Fact {
    key: Integer
    kind: FactKind
    position: sudoku/Position?
    digit: Integer?
    unit: technique/UnitRef?
    source: SourceKind
    supports: Set<Integer>
    confidence: Decimal
    remaining: Set<Integer>?
    checked: Boolean?
    -- One atomic proposition, or coverage: the possibility set of one cell's
    -- nine alternatives or one digit's nine positions in a unit. remaining
    -- and checked are present exactly when kind is coverage: remaining holds
    -- the alternatives not yet folded out; checked is the player's claim
    -- that every source was examined (the cell's twenty peers, or the
    -- unit's nine positions), earned by their own folds or read from a
    -- coverage flag, and it may be false.
    -- A coverage fact is one item however many facts it has folded; supports
    -- names each of them, and they need not stay live. It is a tally, not a
    -- bundle: it does not keep its premises addressable, so it is not a chunk.
    -- supports names earlier trace facts, including fallible ones.
}

value MemoryItem {
    facts: Set<Fact>
    method: technique/Technique?
    refreshed_at: Integer
    -- A raw fact is one item, and a coverage fact is one raw item. Multiple
    -- facts require a learned, recognized chunk of this method; no arbitrary
    -- bundle can be called one chunk.
}

value BranchBelief {
    branch: Integer
    choice: technique/Placement
    alternatives: Set<Integer>
    change_keys: Set<Integer>
    -- Contents count against memory unless explicitly written externally.
    -- The record can be incomplete; no observer checkpoint is retrievable.
}

value Mind {
    memory: Set<MemoryItem>
    focus: Set<sudoku/Position>
    digit: Integer?
    branches: List<BranchBelief>
    pending: Fact?
    confidence: Decimal
    fatigue: Decimal
    frustration: Decimal
    fruitless: Integer
    episode_depth: Integer
    guesses: Integer
    rests: Integer
    perceived_complete: Boolean
}

value Action {
    kind: ActionKind
    mode: ReasoningMode
    positions: Set<sudoku/Position>
    digits: Set<Integer>
    method: technique/Technique?
    premises: Set<Integer>
    intended: Fact?
    source: SourceKind?
}

value Draw {
    index: Integer
    purpose: String
    value: Decimal
}

value Plan {
    action: Action?
    cost: Integer
    draws: List<Draw>
    next_cursor: Integer
}

value Effect {
    sheet: Sheet
    mind: Mind
    observations: Set<Fact>
    draws: List<Draw>
    next_cursor: Integer
    progress: Boolean
    episode_failed: Boolean
}

value Judgement {
    result: ObjectiveResult
    wrong_entries: Set<technique/Placement>
    faulty_inferences: Set<Integer>
    slips: Set<Integer>
    corrections: Set<Integer>
    lucky_guesses: Set<Integer>
    obstacles: Set<Obstacle>
    steps: List<StepJudgement>
    -- Integers name microsteps; these are observer labels, never Mind data.
}

value StepJudgement {
    index: Integer
    perceived_reasoning_valid: Boolean?
    premises_true: Boolean?
    result_correct: Boolean?
    error_noticed: Boolean
    correction_perceived: Boolean
    proof: technique/Deduction?
    matching_methods: Set<technique/Technique>
    dependencies: Set<Integer>
    obstacles: Set<Obstacle>
    -- Null truth/proof fields mean not applicable, not a failed judgement.
    -- proofs are reconstructed from the actual acquired premises, never
    -- substituted with a better proof the player did not find.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Attempts and immutable trace
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Attempt {
    request: AttemptRequest
    assessment: Assessment?
    sample_index: Integer?
    status: ready | acting | ended
    transitions status {
        ready -> acting
        acting -> ready
        ready -> ended
        acting -> ended
        terminal: ended
    }
    sheet: Sheet
    mind: Mind
    spent: Integer
    cursor: Integer
    plan: Plan?
    reason: StopReason?
    judgement: Judgement?
    steps: Microstep with attempt = this
    is_finished: status = ended and judgement != null
}

entity Microstep {
    attempt: Attempt
    index: Integer
    action: Action
    before_sheet: Sheet
    before_mind: Mind
    effect: Effect
    after_sheet: effect.sheet
    after_mind: effect.mind
    cost: Integer
    observations: effect.observations
    draws: List<Draw>
    progress: effect.progress
    episode_failed: effect.episode_failed
}

value AssessmentRequest {
    template: AttemptRequest
    seeds: List<Integer>
    -- Seeds replace template.seed, one occurrence per run. All other inputs
    -- stay fixed. No adaptive stopping or deletion of inconvenient runs.
}

value Distribution {
    samples: List<Integer>
    minimum: Integer?
    median: Decimal?
    p90: Integer?
    maximum: Integer?
}

value AssessmentSummary {
    total: Integer
    solved: Integer
    solve_fraction: Decimal
    successful_effort: Distribution
    unsuccessful_effort: Distribution
    -- Failed attempts carry observed expenditure, not a guessed solve cost.
    report: AssessmentDetail
}

value AssessmentDetail {
    stop_counts: Set<StopCount>
    technique_counts: Set<TechniqueCount>
    obstacle_counts: Set<ObstacleCount>
    mistakes: Distribution
    corrections: Distribution
    guesses: Distribution
    rereads: Distribution
    fruitless_searches: Distribution
    dependency_depth: Distribution
}

value StopCount { reason: StopReason, count: Integer }
value TechniqueCount {
    method: technique/Technique
    recognized: Integer
    derived: Integer
    attempted_faultily: Integer
}
value ObstacleCount { obstacle: Obstacle, count: Integer }

entity Assessment {
    request: AssessmentRequest
    status: gathering | complete
    transitions status {
        gathering -> complete
        terminal: complete
    }
    runs: AssessmentRun with assessment = this
    summary: AssessmentSummary?
    is_ready: runs.count = request.seeds.count and runs.all(r => r.attempt.is_finished)
}

entity AssessmentRun {
    assessment: Assessment
    index: Integer
    attempt: Attempt
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Provisional defaults: all numerical choices are model assumptions
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    model_version: String = "human-solving-1"

    -- technique.allium's, restated as its figures are elsewhere.
    catalogue_version: String = technique/config.catalogue_version

    -- Names the randomness port's seeded stream. The generator is the
    -- port's; a different generator is a different name.
    random_version: String = "seeded-stream-1"
}

default CognitiveAbility standard_ability = {
    capacity: 4, retention: 12, interference: 0.02, attention_cells: 3,
    switch_effort: 2, reasoning_depth: 8, notice: 0.90,
    perception_error: 0.01, inference_error: 0.01, entry_error: 0.005
}
default Biases standard_biases = {
    area_fixation: 0.5, digit_fixation: 0.3, familiar_preference: 2.0,
    confirmation: 0.10, overconfidence: 0.05
}
default Coping standard_coping = {
    note_use: 0.70, verification: 0.35, confidence_threshold: 0.80,
    guess_when_stuck: 0.25, guess_depth: 2, guess_limit: 12,
    branch_recall: 0.80, patience: 24, rest_limit: 2,
    fatigue_gain: 0.0002, frustration_gain: 0.03,
    progress_relief: 0.15, rest_relief: 0.25
}
default Environment paper = {
    manual_notes: true, automatic_candidates: false, highlighting: off,
    feedback: none, uniqueness_disclosed: true, branch_notes: true
}
default EffortCosts standard_costs = { read: 1, think: 2, write: 1, rest: 5, declare: 1 }
default Limits standard_limits = { effort: 5000, microsteps: 10000 }

default Experience rules_only = {
    familiar: {}, fluency: 0.0, chunk_facts: 1, habit: 1.0, overrides: {}
}
default Experience practised_beginner = {
    familiar: {
        technique/full_unit, technique/cross_hatch,
        technique/naked_single, technique/hidden_single
    },
    fluency: 0.55, chunk_facts: 2, habit: 1.0, overrides: {}
}
default Experience intermediate = {
    familiar: {
        technique/full_unit, technique/cross_hatch,
        technique/naked_single, technique/hidden_single,
        technique/pointing, technique/claiming,
        technique/naked_pair, technique/hidden_pair,
        technique/naked_triple, technique/hidden_triple, technique/x_wing
    },
    fluency: 0.75, chunk_facts: 3, habit: 1.0, overrides: {}
}
default Experience expert = {
    familiar: {
        technique/full_unit, technique/cross_hatch,
        technique/naked_single, technique/hidden_single,
        technique/pointing, technique/claiming,
        technique/naked_pair, technique/hidden_pair,
        technique/naked_triple, technique/hidden_triple,
        technique/naked_quad, technique/hidden_quad,
        technique/x_wing, technique/swordfish, technique/jellyfish,
        technique/xy_wing, technique/xyz_wing, technique/w_wing,
        technique/skyscraper, technique/two_string_kite, technique/empty_rectangle,
        technique/simple_colouring, technique/remote_pair, technique/x_chain,
        technique/xy_chain, technique/alternating_inference_chain,
        technique/forcing_chain, technique/unique_rectangle_type_1,
        technique/bug_plus_one
    },
    fluency: 0.95, chunk_facts: 4, habit: 1.0, overrides: {}
}

default PlayerProfile rules_only_novice = {
    name: "Rules-only novice", ability: standard_ability, experience: rules_only,
    biases: standard_biases, coping: standard_coping
}
default PlayerProfile beginner = {
    name: "Practised beginner", ability: standard_ability, experience: practised_beginner,
    biases: standard_biases, coping: standard_coping
}
default PlayerProfile intermediate_player = {
    name: "Intermediate", ability: standard_ability, experience: intermediate,
    biases: standard_biases, coping: standard_coping
}
default PlayerProfile expert_player = {
    name: "Expert", ability: standard_ability, experience: expert,
    biases: standard_biases, coping: standard_coping
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Helpers have the contracts below, not a licence to run a hidden solver.
-- valid_request enforces every declared range, uniqueness of keyed fields,
-- valid Sudoku positions/digits and supported nonempty version identifiers.
-- fresh_sheet retains the immutable givens and copies them into entries;
-- notes and branch_marks are empty. fresh_mind has empty memory/focus/branches,
-- no pending fact/digit, confidence/fatigue/frustration/counters zero and
-- perceived_complete=false. Even printed givens must be attended and read.

rule BeginAttempt {
    when: Simulate(request)
    requires: valid_request(request)
    requires: solution_count(request.givens, sudoku/config.side) = 1
    ensures: Attempt.created(
        request: request, assessment: null, sample_index: null,
        status: ready, sheet: fresh_sheet(request.givens),
        mind: fresh_mind(), spent: 0, cursor: 0,
        plan: null, reason: null, judgement: null
    )
}

rule ChooseMicrostep {
    when: attempt: Attempt.status becomes ready
    requires: attempt.status = ready
    ensures:
        if attempt.steps.count >= attempt.request.limits.microsteps:
            attempt.reason = microstep_limit
            attempt.status = ended
        else if attempt.spent >= attempt.request.limits.effort:
            attempt.reason = effort_limit
            attempt.status = ended
        else:
            let proposed = choose_action(policy_input(attempt.request), attempt.mind, attempt.cursor)
            if proposed.action = null:
                attempt.reason = no_action
                attempt.status = ended
            else if attempt.spent + proposed.cost > attempt.request.limits.effort:
                attempt.reason = effort_limit
                attempt.status = ended
            else:
                attempt.plan = proposed
                attempt.status = acting
}

rule PerformMicrostep {
    when: attempt: Attempt.status transitions_to acting
    requires: attempt.status = acting
    requires: attempt.plan != null

    -- Read before anything changes: the plan is cleared, the sheet and mind
    -- replaced, and the step about to be recorded is not yet among the
    -- steps. A creation parameter or an if guard below would otherwise
    -- read the state the rule leaves.
    let plan = attempt.plan
    let index = attempt.steps.count + 1
    let before_sheet = attempt.sheet
    let before_mind = attempt.mind
    let effect = perform_action(
        policy_input(attempt.request), before_sheet, before_mind,
        plan.action, index, plan.next_cursor
    )
    ensures:
        Microstep.created(
            attempt: attempt, index: index,
            action: plan.action,
            before_sheet: before_sheet, before_mind: before_mind,
            effect: effect, cost: plan.cost,
            draws: concatenate(plan.draws, effect.draws)
        )
        attempt.sheet = effect.sheet
        attempt.mind = effect.mind
        attempt.spent = attempt.spent + plan.cost
        attempt.cursor = effect.next_cursor
        if plan.action.kind = claim_finished:
            attempt.reason = claimed
            attempt.status = ended
        else if plan.action.kind = give_up:
            attempt.reason = abandoned
            attempt.status = ended
        else:
            attempt.status = ready
        attempt.plan = null
}

rule JudgeEndedAttempt {
    when: attempt: Attempt.status transitions_to ended
    requires: attempt.status = ended
    ensures: attempt.judgement = judge(attempt.request, attempt.sheet, attempt.steps)
}

-- Batch run creation uses an indexed request helper rather than editing a
-- completed attempt's seed. Each occurrence gets its own run, even repeats.
rule BeginAssessment {
    when: Assess(request)
    requires: valid_assessment(request)
    requires: solution_count(request.template.givens, sudoku/config.side) = 1
    ensures: Assessment.created(request: request, status: gathering, summary: null)
}

rule StartAssessmentRuns {
    when: assessment: Assessment.created
    ensures:
        for index in indices(assessment.request.seeds):
            Attempt.created(
                request: request_for(assessment.request, index), status: ready,
                assessment: assessment, sample_index: index,
                sheet: fresh_sheet(assessment.request.template.givens),
                mind: fresh_mind(), spent: 0, cursor: 0,
                plan: null, reason: null, judgement: null
            )
}

rule AssociateAssessmentRun {
    when: attempt: Attempt.created
    requires: attempt.assessment != null
    ensures: AssessmentRun.created(
        assessment: attempt.assessment, index: attempt.sample_index, attempt: attempt
    )
}

rule CompleteAssessment {
    when: assessment: Assessment.is_ready
    requires: assessment.status = gathering
    requires: assessment.is_ready
    ensures:
        assessment.summary = summarize(assessment.request, assessment.runs)
        assessment.status = complete
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant EffortIsBounded {
    for attempt in Attempts:
        attempt.spent >= 0 and attempt.spent <= attempt.request.limits.effort
            and attempt.steps.count <= attempt.request.limits.microsteps
}

invariant EveryMicrostepCostsSomething {
    for step in Microsteps:
        step.cost >= 1 and step.index >= 1
            and step.index <= step.attempt.steps.count
}

invariant TraceIndicesAreUnique {
    for a in Microsteps:
        for b in Microsteps:
            a != b and a.attempt = b.attempt implies a.index != b.index
}

invariant StateIsBounded {
    for attempt in Attempts:
        attempt.mind.fatigue >= 0 and attempt.mind.fatigue <= 1
            and attempt.mind.frustration >= 0 and attempt.mind.frustration <= 1
            and attempt.mind.focus.count <= attempt.request.profile.ability.attention_cells
            and retained_chunks(attempt.mind) <= attempt.request.profile.ability.capacity
            and attempt.mind.guesses <= attempt.request.profile.coping.guess_limit
            and attempt.mind.branches.count <= attempt.request.profile.coping.guess_depth
            and attempt.mind.rests <= attempt.request.profile.coping.rest_limit
}

invariant GivensRemain {
    for attempt in Attempts:
        attempt.sheet.givens = attempt.request.givens
            and attempt.request.givens.all(g => attempt.sheet.entries.any(e =>
                e.position = g.position and e.digit = g.digit))
}

invariant EndHasAReason {
    for attempt in Attempts:
        attempt.status = ended implies attempt.reason != null
}

invariant AssessmentIsComplete {
    for assessment in Assessments:
        assessment.status = complete implies assessment.is_ready and assessment.summary != null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Boundaries and detailed behavioural contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract CognitiveOperations {
    choose_action: (input: PolicyInput, mind: Mind, cursor: Integer) -> Plan
    perform_action: (
        input: PolicyInput, sheet: Sheet, mind: Mind,
        action: Action, index: Integer, cursor: Integer
    ) -> Effect

    @invariant ProjectedObservation
        -- choose_action has no access to Sheet. perform_action can read
        -- sheet contents only to execute the specified observation or edit.
        -- Immutable givens identify protected entries. Only an explicitly
        -- requested solution_check may use those givens to obtain the aid's
        -- boolean error signal; it cannot return a solution or extra facts.
        -- All cognitive effects obey Simulation's guarantees; returning an
        -- entire hidden solution as one Effect violates this contract.
}

-- One player, four models. reach, effort and lapse read technique.allium's
-- Profile, so a player described here is put to them through this.
contract Projection {
    coarse_profile: (
        profile: PlayerProfile, environment: Environment, limits: Limits
    ) -> technique/Profile

    @invariant ProjectionToTheCoarseProfile
        -- What is projected, field by field:
        --   repertoire: experience.familiar.
        --   capacity: ability.capacity.
        --   marking: full_marks if the environment has manual notes or
        --     automatic candidates, else no_marks.
        --   fixation: unfixed if area_fixation and digit_fixation are both
        --     zero; else nearby if area_fixation is the greater or they are
        --     equal; else same_digit.
        --   patience: coping.patience.
        -- Five fields have nothing here to be read from and take a stated
        -- provisional figure, each an open question below: spans, every
        -- extent; order, systematic; upkeep, every kind of unit; budget,
        -- limits.effort; fatigue, limits.effort, so that capacity never
        -- falls within budget.
        -- The projection reads nothing else and is the same every time. It
        -- loses what the Profile cannot say: chance, fluency, partial notes,
        -- and elementary reasoning, so that rules_only projects to an empty
        -- repertoire and stalls in reach.allium at once while the same
        -- player here can still derive a single.
}

surface Simulation {
    contracts:
        fulfils CognitiveOperations
        fulfils Projection

    provides:
        Simulate(request)

    @guarantee InputValidation
        -- Reject an invalid request before creating an attempt. This includes
        -- malformed/duplicate/out-of-range givens, a puzzle with zero or
        -- multiple solutions, versions other than config's supported values,
        -- bad ranges, duplicate method
        -- overrides and branch_notes enabled without manual_notes.
        -- No request can supply notes, entries beyond givens, memories or
        -- observer truth. Snapshot the complete effective input; later edits
        -- to a preset cannot alter an existing attempt.

    @guarantee MicrostepsAreTheUnitOfWork
        -- choose_action only sees the profile/environment/costs, bounded
        -- Mind and random stream. policy_input drops the givens. Board layout
        -- and Sudoku rules are long-term knowledge available to all profiles.
        -- It cannot enumerate true deductions and pick an affordable one.
        -- perform_action reads only the source and positions targeted by
        -- that action. No action combines a scan with unrecorded inference,
        -- notes, placement or upkeep. A failed action still costs effort.
        -- The following guarantees completely constrain these two helpers;
        -- strategy changes within them require a new model_version.

    @guarantee ObservationAndAttention
        -- attend selects at most attention_cells positions and optionally a
        -- digit, without reading cell contents. Each inspect reads ONE
        -- entry/empty fact, one candidate mark, one coverage flag, or one
        -- enabled aid/feedback fact at a focused position. Whole-unit
        -- inspection needs successive such events. Reinspection is paid.
        -- A notice draw succeeds with notice/(1+fatigue). If noticed, an
        -- independent perception-error draw uses min(1,p*(1+fatigue)).
        -- A misread substitutes a different digit 1..9 or reverses a
        -- candidate/coverage assertion, chosen uniformly among valid
        -- alternatives. It never changes the sheet or a given itself.
        -- Unnoticed facts add no belief. Observations record the presented
        -- source and perceived fact separately through before_sheet and
        -- observations; their truth remains observer information.

    @guarantee MemoryAndChunking
        -- All retained puzzle propositions, pending conclusions, unfinished
        -- proofs and unwritten branch data count against capacity. A focus
        -- address and control counters are metadata, not stored board facts.
        -- Raw facts consume one chunk each. A coverage fact consumes one
        -- whatever it has folded; a fact folded into it may be evicted
        -- without changing the set, and which sources it has folded is
        -- bookkeeping the set carries, like a control counter, not further
        -- facts. A multi-fact chunk must match a
        -- previously recognized familiar pattern, contain at most the
        -- effective skill's chunk_facts, and preserve individually addressable
        -- premises. A phrase like "the whole grid" cannot be one chunk.
        -- Familiar recognition may use a matching chunk without expanding
        -- all its facts at once. An operation that needs to unpack facts
        -- counts the expanded live items against capacity as well.
        -- Overflow evicts the least recently refreshed item, breaking ties
        -- by the smallest fact key; trace the eviction, never increase capacity.
        -- After each microstep, expire items older than retention steps
        -- since refresh. On a focus change, independently lose each remaining
        -- item with probability min(1,interference*(1+fatigue)).
        -- Recall succeeds only from retained memory or by a new paid read
        -- of an external record. Rehearsal refreshes one retained item and
        -- consumes a microstep. There is no free episodic trace lookup.

    @guarantee NotesAreExternalAndFallible
        -- A fresh manual sheet has no notes. One write_note/erase_note edits
        -- one cell-digit mark, coverage flag, or branch checkpoint fragment.
        -- The intended edit uses a retained fact, then entry_error may
        -- substitute one other legal edit. Given entries cannot be edited.
        -- Writing a mark needs a possible fact or a coverage fact that
        -- still holds that digit, checked or not, so marks may be partial;
        -- erasing a mark needs an excluded fact or a checked coverage fact
        -- that no longer holds that digit. Writing a coverage flag needs a
        -- retained checked coverage fact for that cell whose remaining set
        -- is exactly the marks the cell already shows. The flag certifies
        -- the marks as complete, so an incomplete cell cannot be flagged,
        -- and a false flag arises only through an error already in the
        -- facts it and its marks were written from, or a later board change.
        -- Reading a coverage flag yields a coverage fact with checked true,
        -- remaining full and no mark yet folded. A present mark read yields
        -- a possible fact; an absent mark read yields an excluded fact from
        -- manual_note only while a checked coverage fact for that cell is
        -- retained, else nothing. Folding each such fact settles that
        -- alternative, present stays and absent leaves, and which marks
        -- have been folded is the set's bookkeeping. Missing manual marks
        -- mean unknown until such a coverage fact has folded every mark.
        -- Partial positive marks alone never establish excluded candidates
        -- or strong links.
        -- A player may falsely believe coverage through an error; resulting
        -- moves are attempted inferences, not sound catalogue deductions.
        -- Changing an entry never silently repairs manual notes or memory.
        -- Old coverage remains the player's stale assertion until checked.
        -- Every use of an external fact requires reading it into memory.

    @guarantee AidsChangeTheEnvironment
        -- Automatic candidates, if enabled, show exactly digits not excluded
        -- by currently entered peers. They do not consult the solution and
        -- can therefore reflect a wrong entry. They are distinct from
        -- manual notes and recalculate after edits, but must still be read.
        -- Highlighting reveals only the selected cell's peers and/or visible
        -- occurrences of a selected digit. It supplies no deductions.
        -- Conflict feedback reveals equal peer entries; solution_check may
        -- reveal that a queried entry disagrees with the certified solution,
        -- never the correct digit. Each feedback query is a paid inspection.
        -- The aid checks the original Sheet.givens, never a puzzle that
        -- silently treats the player's later entries as additional givens.
        -- The cognitive operation receives only the requested boolean; the
        -- full solution and observer judgement remain inaccessible to it.
        -- With feedback=none the evaluator never interrupts a mistake.
        -- uniqueness_disclosed exposes only the setter's uniqueness promise,
        -- as a fact to read; it supplies no other solution information.

    @guarantee FamiliarRecognition
        -- recognize tests one chosen familiar method on the premises in
        -- memory. All presence, absence and coverage claims needed by that
        -- particular witness must have been acquired or derived. Successful
        -- recognition has probability fluency/(1+fatigue); failure is paid
        -- and yields no conclusion. Recognition cannot search unseen units.
        -- A successful recognition proposes the catalogue conclusion if
        -- the perceived premises fit. An inference-error draw may instead
        -- omit one required premise or confuse one digit/target, recording
        -- that operation. Its correctness is judged separately afterward.
        -- A recognize action may instead group a fully acquired fragment of
        -- the chosen familiar pattern: a cell's checked candidate set, a
        -- digit's checked places in one unit, a witnessed link, or a
        -- consecutive implication path. The fragment has no missing premise
        -- and fits chunk_facts. It uses the same fluency/error draws, costs
        -- think, and yields a chunk rather than a placement or strike.
        -- This permits incremental chunk construction before attempting a
        -- larger pattern. No chunk can be formed just because that larger
        -- pattern would exist on the unseen sheet. Raw and expanded facts
        -- still fit capacity while the grouping operation is performed.
        -- The ladder supplies a search preference, not universal discovery
        -- or a guarantee that a familiar method will be noticed.

    @guarantee BoundedElementaryReasoning
        -- Every profile knows the rules. infer performs ONE paid move,
        -- of three kinds. Construct: a cell's coverage fact from a retained
        -- empty fact for that cell, or a unit-digit's from public geometry;
        -- all nine alternatives remain and no source is folded, and the
        -- empty fact enters supports and need not stay live. A set is
        -- never inferred from marks alone. Fold: one live premise settles
        -- one alternative or one source: a peer's placement removes its
        -- digit from a cell's set; a position holding another digit, or
        -- excluded by a peer holding the digit, leaves a unit-digit set; a
        -- placement of the digit inside the unit ends that set with nothing
        -- to find; an empty peer folds with no removal. Conclude, one
        -- implication: a known placement excludes that digit from a peer;
        -- one remaining alternative asserts it; nothing
        -- remaining is the contradiction GuessesAndRecovery names; k checked
        -- sets of k positions restricted to k digits reserve those digits;
        -- a verified weak/strong link carries one literal implication; or
        -- completed exhaustive cases yield their common conclusion.
        -- Every membership, exclusion, coverage and exhaustiveness premise
        -- must be live, in retained facts or an eligible learned chunk, at
        -- the step that consumes it. A coverage fact stands for what was
        -- folded into it, and the observer judges a conclusion drawn from
        -- one through the closure of its supports, so a folded misread
        -- still taints it. Comparing one pair or adding one implication is
        -- likewise a separate paid action. Reasoning depth bounds a chain:
        -- consecutive implications each taking the previous conclusion as
        -- a premise. Constructing and folding are not chain steps; capacity,
        -- retention, interference and effort bound them instead, and
        -- capacity still bounds simultaneously live premises.
        -- A bound failure yields reasoning_limit, not a forced answer.
        -- inference_error uses min(1,p*(1+fatigue)) and the same explicit
        -- premise/digit/target fault operations as recognition.
        -- Unfamiliar named techniques may emerge from these steps. Match a
        -- completed sound proof to the catalogue afterward for reporting;
        -- do not call the unfamiliar recognizer to generate that proof.
        -- This does not add the method to experience or enlarge chunks.
        -- A derivation without a named match is reported as elementary
        -- reasoning, with its proof intact, never mislabeled as a technique.

    @guarantee ChoiceAndBias
        -- The initial policy searches cells, digits and units, not deductions.
        -- At each choice it enumerates eligible local actions from Mind:
        -- reading missing premises, trying a familiar method, folding a live
        -- fact into a coverage fact, extending an elementary episode, noting
        -- a retained fact, checking a proposal,
        -- committing a sufficiently confident proposal, or changing focus.
        -- Eligibility may use public geometry and retained facts only.
        -- Base weights are 1; a familiar-method action uses skill.habit.
        -- Multiply by (1+area_fixation) when staying in the current area,
        -- (1+digit_fixation) when staying on its digit, and
        -- (1+familiar_preference*fluency) for a familiar-method attempt.
        -- None applies without a previous focus/digit/familiar method.
        -- At a fresh search episode, a familiar method's base weight is also
        -- divided by its catalogue rank, encouraging cheap methods first.
        -- Draw one action proportional to these weights in canonical order.
        -- Recognition failure does not establish that no pattern exists.
        -- A confirmation draw may skip a selected check of opposing evidence;
        -- the action still costs effort and logs the skipped check.
        -- Perceived confidence starts at notice for a read fact; a conclusion
        -- takes the minimum premise confidence (zero with missing premises),
        -- then adds overconfidence, clamped to [0,1]. It never uses truth.
        -- Before committing, request a verification episode with probability
        -- verification*(1-overconfidence). That episode rereads prerequisites
        -- in separate inspect steps; verify compares only retained claims.
        -- A commitment requires confidence >= confidence_threshold.
        -- note_use gates externalization when allowed. All biases set to
        -- zero remove their own effects, not all stochastic behaviour.

    @guarantee GuessesAndRecovery
        -- A speculative guess has no complete proof. Only at patience limit,
        -- with remaining depth and guess budgets, may guess_when_stuck choose
        -- a branch. Select a perceived open cell with at least two perceived
        -- alternatives by the ordinary attention weights; choose an untried
        -- alternative uniformly. It need not be a true candidate.
        -- Increment guesses and retain/write the branch choice and intended
        -- alternatives before committing it. Recording each checkpoint
        -- fragment is separately paid; branch data uses memory or branch_notes.
        -- Bounded forcing proofs that never commit speculation are infer
        -- actions; they remain separate from guess events.
        -- A contradiction must be noticed: observed equal peer entries,
        -- a coverage fact with nothing remaining, or explicit permitted
        -- feedback. The observer cannot trigger repair.
        -- Recovery chooses the newest accessible branch or a suspected
        -- erroneous entry/mark. backtrack recalls one recorded change; an
        -- unwritten change is recalled correctly with branch_recall, else
        -- omitted or misremembered. Actual restoration uses separate paid
        -- erase/enter/note events and can slip. No snapshot restore is free.
        -- Repair invalidates only dependencies the player notices. Wrong
        -- dependent beliefs can persist. Observed correction does not
        -- retroactively make a faulty inference sound or a lucky guess logical.

    @guarantee FatigueFrustrationAndStopping
        -- For every non-rest action, fatigue increases by cost*fatigue_gain,
        -- capped at 1. A fruitless episode is one failed recognition, an
        -- elementary episode ended without a usable conclusion, or a
        -- planned scan completed with no noticed lead; count it once.
        -- It adds frustration_gain to frustration and one to fruitless.
        -- A fold that removes nothing continues its episode and is not
        -- fruitless by itself.
        -- A noticed error also adds frustration_gain, not a search failure.
        -- Perceived progress resets fruitless, clears episode_depth and
        -- subtracts progress_relief from frustration, floored at zero.
        -- Such progress may be objectively wrong. Truth never resets counters.
        -- Frustration reduces effective patience to
        -- max(1,ceil(patience*(1-frustration/2))). All references to patience
        -- limit, including branch eligibility, use this effective value.
        -- At patience limit: attempt a permitted guess with its stated
        -- probability, otherwise rest if available and either fatigue or
        -- frustration is positive, otherwise give_up. A rest subtracts
        -- rest_relief from both (floor zero), resets fruitless and increments
        -- rests. Rest does not refund effort, restore memory or teach skills.
        -- All ordinary effects use the before-action state; fatigue,
        -- frustration, expiration and capacity eviction follow the action.
        -- Zero fatigue_gain/frustration_gain from a fresh mind disables each.
        -- A player may claim_finished only from a retained belief of all
        -- cells filled and a paid completion-check episode; this belief can
        -- be mistaken. The observer does not end a correct grid early.
        -- No eligible action gives no_action. Limits take precedence before
        -- the next action; a budget-rejected action has no physical or mental
        -- effect. Every attempt ends after finitely many positive-cost steps.

    @guarantee EffortAccounting
        -- inspect, recall, rehearse cost read. attend costs read plus
        -- switch_effort iff focus or digit changes. recognize, infer and
        -- verify cost think; enter, erase_entry, write_note, erase_note cost
        -- write. guess costs think+write; backtrack costs think for recalling
        -- one change (the edit follows separately). rest costs rest;
        -- claim_finished/give_up cost declare. No action has zero cost.
        -- spent is the sum of completed microstep costs. Choice evaluation,
        -- observer analysis and batch aggregation add no player effort.
        -- The costs are abstract units, not seconds. Per-method effort
        -- emerges from actual reads, proof steps, notes, misses and repairs.
}

surface AttemptResult {
    context attempt: Attempt
    exposes:
        attempt.request
        attempt.assessment
        attempt.sample_index
        attempt.status
        attempt.sheet
        attempt.mind
        attempt.spent
        attempt.cursor
        attempt.reason
        attempt.judgement
        for step in attempt.steps:
            step.index
            step.action
            step.before_sheet
            step.after_sheet
            step.before_mind
            step.after_mind
            step.cost
            step.observations
            step.draws
            step.progress
            step.episode_failed

    @guarantee ExactReplay
        -- Identical givens, resolved profile/environment/costs/limits, seed,
        -- model_version, catalogue_version and random_version yield identical
        -- action order, draws, state changes, effort and outcome, disregarding
        -- run identifiers. Record all these inputs and every consumed draw.
        -- Draws are the randomness port's: a stream of u in [0,1), begun
        -- from the seed, indexed from zero, the same for the same seed and
        -- random_version. Which generator is the port's and not stated
        -- here; a test supplies the draws through the port's fake.
        -- A Bernoulli outcome is u<p; a uniform n-way index is
        -- floor(u*n); weighted choice is the first cumulative interval
        -- containing u*sum(weights), intervals closed left/open right.
        -- Draw even for p=0 or p=1. Selection gates run note, verification,
        -- confirmation, guess, then weighted choice when applicable;
        -- execution draws notice, perception, recognition, inference,
        -- entry, branch recall, then interference in increasing fact-key
        -- order, only where the action needs that operation.
        -- Probabilities and weights use exact specified decimal/rational
        -- arithmetic for comparisons; no platform rounding changes a choice.
        -- No wall clock, platform iteration order or hidden random
        -- source may affect a run. Unsupported versions are rejected.
        -- Canonical action order is kind declaration order, row, column,
        -- digit, technique rank, then premise key. Compound position/digit
        -- sets use lexicographically sorted members for this comparison.
        -- Zero/one probabilities have no stochastic effect.
        -- A stopped selection's uncommitted draws need not enter the trace:
        -- replay derives the same stopping decision from the same cursor.

    @guarantee ObserverIsSeparate
        -- judge can read full provenance and exact puzzle solutions only
        -- after the attempt ends. Solved means all 81 cells filled without
        -- conflict and every given preserved. Erroneous means at least one
        -- standing entry differs from the unique solution; otherwise an
        -- unfinished grid is incomplete. A mistaken claim is not success.
        -- Annotate each inferred/entered conclusion independently for valid
        -- perceived reasoning, factual premise truth and correct result.
        -- A correct result from faulty premises is not a sound deduction.
        -- A lucky guess stays speculative. Count faults at their originating
        -- event, separately from later wrong actions depending on that event.
        -- Slip means actual edit differs from intended edit. Correction means
        -- a later action removes a recorded false entry/belief, whether or not
        -- the player knew it was false. Expose perceived correction separately.
        -- Obstacles may be multiple; associate each with event and supporting
        -- facts. catalogue_limit is an evaluator conclusion, not a claim
        -- that the puzzle is unsolvable. Stopped never means unsolvable.

    @guarantee CausalTrace
        -- Immutable sequential before/after values reconstruct every physical
        -- and mental change. Keys identify observations and conclusions;
        -- supports refer only to earlier facts. No inference cites a future,
        -- forgotten, uninspected or observer-only premise as available.
        -- Retain failed searches, missed observations, evictions, rereads,
        -- intended versus actual edits, guesses and observed contradictions.
        -- A completed logical move reports its proof and matched catalogue
        -- name(s); the actually attempted familiar method is distinguished
        -- from later observer classification. Its dependency graph separates
        -- enabling moves from search overhead; it is not an oracle policy.
}

surface AssessmentService {
    provides:
        Assess(request)

    @guarantee DeclaredSample
        -- valid_assessment validates the template and a nonempty list of at
        -- most 10000 in-range seeds. Run exactly one fresh attempt per seed
        -- occurrence, with no carry-over memory, fatigue or skill learning.
        -- Expose the ordered seeds, repetitions and actual run count.
        -- Repeated seeds yield repeated runs, not independent evidence.

    @guarantee DistributionDefinitions
        -- total is the declared occurrence count, solved counts objective
        -- solved results, solve_fraction=solved/total using decimal division.
        -- successful_effort contains spent only for solved attempts;
        -- unsuccessful_effort contains spent only for all other attempts,
        -- stratified by stop reason in the associated runs. Failed costs
        -- are observed expenditure, censored with respect to solving effort.
        -- Distribution samples retain run order; extrema and percentiles
        -- use sorted copies. Median averages the central two for even n.
        -- p90 is nearest rank ceil(0.9*n). All four summaries are null for
        -- no samples; a zero solve fraction has no successful effort estimate.
        -- Stop counts partition runs; obstacle counts may overlap. Mistakes
        -- count originating faults, corrections/guesses/rereads/fruitless
        -- searches count the corresponding trace events once. Dependency
        -- depth is the longest chain of sound applied deductions, ignoring
        -- observation/maintenance nodes; report zero when there are none.
        -- Technique counts distinguish familiar recognition, elementary
        -- derivation and faulty attempts; overlapping valid names of one
        -- proof do not multiply its count. Use the attempted name, else the
        -- lowest-rank matching name, and retain all alternatives in its trace.

    @guarantee Interpretation
        -- Comparisons name puzzle, profile, aids, budgets, all versions and
        -- seeds. Never pool differing conditions as one profile's rating.
        -- Report distributions/counts rather than a mandatory scalar score.
        -- A simulated solve fraction is descriptive of these runs, not an
        -- empirically calibrated probability for a human population.
        -- Capacity and experience may be varied independently. Neither a
        -- stronger profile nor more aids must win every individual seed.
}

surface AssessmentResult {
    context assessment: Assessment
    exposes:
        assessment.request
        assessment.status
        assessment.summary
        for run in assessment.runs:
            run.index
            run.attempt
}

surface Acceptance {
    @guarantee ReplayAndIndependence
        -- Replay one complete request twice: all trace content and results
        -- agree. Repeat a seed in an assessment: both occurrences survive.
        -- Cross rules_only and expert with capacities 2 and 6: repertoire
        -- stays fixed when only capacity changes, and capacity stays fixed
        -- when only experience changes. With no errors, a ready familiar
        -- pattern may be recognized; an unfamiliar one requires elementary
        -- steps and may be completed within its bounds without being learned.

    @guarantee ElementaryDeductionWithinCapacity
        -- With every error and interference rate zero, notice 1 and standard
        -- retention, rules_only_novice on paper derives the last digit of a
        -- valid puzzle with eighty givens, by the cell's set and by a unit's
        -- set for that digit. No step needs more than two items live: the
        -- coverage fact with one inspected entry, or with the pending
        -- conclusion. The derivation completes with capacity 2, and no
        -- episode exceeds depth one. A fact folded early may be evicted
        -- before the conclusion without changing it. With capacity 1 the
        -- same derivation stops at capacity_limit or forgotten, since the
        -- set and its next premise cannot both be live. Every profile has
        -- this route; experience adds recognition, not the rules.

    @guarantee PresetsMeetTheCoarsePresets
        -- expert_player under paper projects to technique/expert's
        -- repertoire, capacity and marking, and beginner to
        -- technique/novice's repertoire. Changing only ability.capacity
        -- changes only the projected capacity, and changing only
        -- experience.familiar only the repertoire.

    @guarantee PartialNotesAndHiddenInformation
        -- A cell with only a written 4 but no coverage claim cannot produce
        -- a sound naked single. With a retained checked set {2,5}, writing
        -- the 2 alone, the flag cannot be written; once both marks stand
        -- the flag is written, and a later reread folds every mark back to
        -- {2,5}. No error-free sequence of note edits yields a single from
        -- the 2 alone. Reading another cell must not reveal its
        -- candidates. Observer knowledge of an available X-Wing must not
        -- influence choice before its relevant facts are acquired.
        -- Manual notes can stay stale after a placement; automatic notes
        -- update only when enabled and still require inspection.

    @guarantee ErrorsRecoveryAndAblation
        -- Force a misread, faulty inference and entry slip separately with
        -- controlled random draws. Each has its distinct provenance and
        -- downstream beliefs; none triggers correction without observation.
        -- A later verification can detect and repair it at explicit cost.
        -- A guessed solution digit is labelled a lucky guess, not deduction.
        -- Capacity overflow evicts rather than silently enlarging memory.
        -- With all error/interference rates zero, notice=1 and sufficient
        -- retention, those error sources disappear. Zero biases and zero
        -- fatigue/frustration gains remove just their respective effects.

    @guarantee BoundsAndAssessment
        -- A repetitive focus, forgotten proof, repeated rest or failed repair
        -- still ends at a finite bound. A cost exceeding remaining effort
        -- is not charged or partly executed. Stopping preserves its reason.
        -- False claimed completion is unsuccessful; budget exhaustion is
        -- not an unsolvability verdict. Zero successes yields an empty
        -- successful distribution, not zero effort. Aggregate every declared
        -- run and retain successful and unsuccessful traces.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Every numerical default here is a model assumption and none is calibrated: abilities, biases, coping, costs, limits and the four presets' fluency and chunk sizes. What evidence would move one, and which are fixed first? explanation/human-solving.md owns the route to validation."

open question "The projection gives spans every extent, order systematic and upkeep every kind of unit, because nothing here says otherwise. Should attention_cells bound spans, should the fixations or familiar_preference make an order unsystematic, and should note_use or automatic candidates decide upkeep?"

open question "The projection reads budget from limits.effort and gives fatigue the same figure so that it never bites. effort.allium's unit is a base figure per deduction and this module's is a microstep cost; are the two commensurable at all, or should budget and fatigue be left to whoever asks for a price?"

open question "The presets do not quite meet. technique/novice holds three and keeps no marks; beginner holds four on paper with notes. technique/expert is unfixed; expert_player has the standard biases and projects to nearby. Should one side move, or are novice and beginner different people?"

open question "rules_only projects to an empty repertoire, which reach.allium stalls on at once, though this model lets the same player derive singles by elementary reasoning. Should the projection credit a floor of techniques every rule-knower can derive, and which?"

open question "PlayerProfile, Environment and the projection live here, so anything that wants a hint from reach.allium for one of these presets imports this whole module. Should the three move to a small module both import, once something needs them?"
